## First steps

1. **Set the image.** `kubernetes/flux/deployment.yaml` ships
   `{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace | lower }}/{{ app_slug }}:REPLACE-ME` — a tag no
   registry has, so the first reconcile `ImagePullBackOff`s.
{%- if enable_image_build and ci_shape != 'none' %}
   The usual value is the `:<short-sha>` the CI build pushes. Replace the
   placeholder `Dockerfile` with your service's real build, keeping it non-root
   (UID 65532) and read-only-rootfs friendly.
{%- if ci_shape == 'github' and registry_host != 'ghcr.io' %}
   The vendored build workflow publishes to `ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>` from its
   own literals, so it does NOT push to `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:`
   at the GHCR path, or change the workflow's login step and its
   `REGISTRY`/`IMAGE_NAME`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %}
   The build job rebuilds on every commit. Narrow it with a `changes:` input on
   the `docker-build` include once your source layout is settled.
{%- if not (registry_host.split(':')[0] == git_host or registry_host.split(':')[0].endswith('.' ~ git_host)) %}
   It pushes `$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE:<short-sha>` — this project's GitLab registry,
   not `{{ registry_host }}`. Point `image:` at the project registry, or pass
   `registry:`, `login_registry:`, `login_user:` and `login_password:` to the
   `docker-build` include.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- elif enable_image_build %}
   This repo has no pipeline, so the image is yours to build and push:
   `task build` produces `{{ app_slug }}:dev` locally, and you tag and push it
   to `{{ registry_host }}/{{ git_namespace | lower }}/{{ app_slug }}` yourself. Replace
   the placeholder `Dockerfile` with your service's real build, keeping it
   non-root (UID 65532) and read-only-rootfs friendly.
{%- else %}
   Point it at the upstream image this service runs.
{%- endif %}
   Tags are **literal pins** — there is no dependency bot, so bump them in a
   {{ change_request }}.
2. **Get wired.** Hand your operator [`docs/ONBOARDING.md`](docs/ONBOARDING.md);
   the namespace, secret store, Git source and Kustomization are one file on
   their side.
3. **Ship.** `task lint`, then branch → {{ change_request }}. Flux deploys on
   merge.

## Local development

Needs `task` (go-task), plus `kustomize`, `kubeconform`, `yamllint`, `ruff`,
`python3` (with `pyyaml`, for the egress-fence, scrape-wiring and kustomization gates{% if ci_shape == 'gitlab_selfhosted' %} and the library-pin gate{% endif %}) and `pre-commit`.

```bash
task lint            # every local gate
task render          # print the manifests Flux will apply
{% if enable_image_build %}task build           # docker build
{% endif %}task flux:status     # reconcile state (read-only; needs a kubeconfig)
```

Install the pre-commit hooks (`pre-commit install`) to catch secrets, YAML
problems, a broken doc link, an opened egress fence, a drifted scrape pair and
an emptied kustomization list before you push.
{%- if ci_shape == 'none' %}

There is no pipeline in this repo (CI shape `none`), so `task lint` and the
pre-commit hooks are the whole gate.
{%- endif %}

## Secrets
{% if secrets_backend == 'none' %}
This repo has no secret backend. If the app grows one, add an `ExternalSecret`
referencing the `ClusterSecretStore` the operator provisions — never a value in
git. [`docs/ONBOARDING.md`](docs/ONBOARDING.md) has both backends.
{%- else %}
Secret **values** never go in git. `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret.yaml`
references the `ClusterSecretStore` `{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}`, which
the operator creates.
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %}
Items live in the shared vault prefixed with the slug, so `remoteRef.key` is
`"{{ app_slug }}: <Item>"` and `remoteRef.property` is the field.
{%- else %}
Each masked CI/CD variable on this project maps across as a `remoteRef.key`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

